---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Contact — Freightgcc" description="Contact Freightgcc Sales, Admin and Editorial: quotation enquiries, account and privacy questions, corrections, research and republication requests.">
  <article>
    <h1>Contact</h1>
    <p class="article-meta">Sales · Admin · Editorial · Last reviewed 16 November 2026</p>

    <p class="lede">
      Use the department that matches your question. Commercial and quotation enquiries go to Sales;
      corrections, research and republication requests go to Editorial; account, privacy and data
      requests go to Admin.
    </p>

    <h2>Departments</h2>
    <div class="contact-grid">
      <section class="contact-card">
        <h2>Sales</h2>
        <p><a href="mailto:inquiry@freightgcc.com">inquiry@freightgcc.com</a></p>
        <p>Commercial enquiries, quotation requests, supplier introductions and partnership discussions.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="contact-card">
        <h2>Admin</h2>
        <p><a href="mailto:admin@freightgcc.com">admin@freightgcc.com</a></p>
        <p>Accounts, invoicing, privacy requests and data-correction requests.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="contact-card">
        <h2>Editorial</h2>
        <p><a href="mailto:martin@freightgcc.com">martin@freightgcc.com</a></p>
        <p>Corrections, factual challenges, source suggestions, research questions and republication permission.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
    <p>
      <a class="btn-whatsapp" href="https://wa.me/8613323237275" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        Contact us now
      </a>
      <span class="contact-note">WhatsApp: +86 133 2323 7275</span>
    </p>
    <p>
      For editorial mail, put the category in the subject — <em>Correction</em>, <em>Republication</em>,
      <em>Accessibility</em>, <em>Security</em> — so the message reaches the right queue. Mail is read on
      working days, without out-of-hours cover.
    </p>

    <h2>What Editorial can answer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Editorial questions.</strong> Why a page reaches a judgement, how a range was built, and which source supports a figure.</li>
      <li><strong>Source suggestions.</strong> A customs notice, tariff schedule or port circular we have missed. If it holds up, it is cited in the page and dated in the update log.</li>
      <li><strong>Corrections.</strong> A wrong number, a misnamed authority or a dead link, handled under the <a href="/corrections/">corrections policy</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Republication and media.</strong> Quoting at length, translation, syndication, or comment on a sector.</li>
      <li><strong>Accessibility and privacy.</strong> Barrier reports and data requests, handled under their own policies.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What Editorial cannot answer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Quotations and bookings.</strong> Editorial does not quote freight rates, nominate vessels or take bookings. Use Sales for a commercial enquiry; any rate or booking is subject to written confirmation.</li>
      <li><strong>Customs rulings.</strong> We cannot classify goods, issue a ruling, or predict how an officer will treat a specific entry.</li>
      <li><strong>Shipment-specific advice.</strong> Whether a consignment will clear, what a certificate must say for your product, or what to do about a container already held at a port.</li>
      <li><strong>Legal, tax or investment advice.</strong> The pages are editorial analysis; a qualified local adviser is the right source for a decision.</li>
      <li><strong>Editorial recommendations.</strong> Editorial does not rate or vet suppliers; commercial introductions are handled by Sales and disclosed as commercial.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Out-of-scope questions get a straight no, not a stretched answer.</p>

    <h2>What to include</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The page URL</strong>, not just the title.</li>
      <li><strong>The sentence or table row</strong>, quoted, so there is no doubt which claim is in question.</li>
      <li><strong>The source that contradicts it</strong>, with a link and, for a PDF, the page number.</li>
      <li><strong>The outcome you want</strong>: correction, explanation, or a reply you can quote.</li>
      <li><strong>Whether your name may be published</strong>, and any deadline with the reason for it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Response times</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Message</th><th scope="col">Target</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Any first reply</td><td>Five working days</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A confirmed factual error fixed</td><td>Ten working days from confirmation</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Republication or media decision</td><td>Ten working days</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Accessibility barrier acknowledged</td><td>Five working days; the fix date follows in the reply</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Privacy request</td><td>Within 30 days</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="meta">
      These are targets, not guarantees. A question needing a source checked can take longer; if a reply
      will miss the target, the acknowledgement says when to expect it.
    </p>

    <h2>Languages</h2>
    <p>
      The site and the desk work in English, and an English message gets the most precise reply. Messages
      in other languages are accepted and read as well as they can be, but answered in English. Where a
      point rests on Arabic or Chinese wording, send the original with an English summary.
    </p>

    <h2>Media and republication</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Short quotation</strong> with attribution and a link needs no permission.</li>
      <li><strong>Full reproduction, translation or syndication</strong> needs a written yes first: send the outlet, page, intended use and whether it will be edited or paywalled.</li>
      <li><strong>Images and data</strong> are not licensed by default, and charts built on a third party's data carry that party's terms. Corrections travel with a republished piece.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Security reports</h2>
    <p>
      Use the same address with <em>Security</em> in the subject: the affected URL, what an attacker could
      do, and the steps to reproduce it. Please do not degrade the service, access data that is not yours,
      or test a third party's systems. There is no bug bounty; reports are handled in good faith,
      acknowledged within five working days, and given a reasonable window to fix before publication. Most
      of the surface is static: no login, no database, no stored user content.
    </p>

    <h2>Postal and company details</h2>
    <p>
      Freightgcc is published by <strong>YIWU DINGWEI TRADING COMPANY</strong>, 3# Building, North Silicon
      Valley, Qiaodong District, Zhangjiakou City, Hebei, China. D-U-N-S Number: <strong>585974555</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Email and the WhatsApp number above are the verified channels. Treat any other address, phone
      number, invoice or registration number presented as Freightgcc's as unverified — the desk will never
      ask for payment, card details or credentials.
    </p>

